{
  "content": "- **Reconstruct the active user contract cumulatively from user turns only, with the latest turn treated as the newest constraint update.** Earlier user requirements remain in force unless the user clearly narrows, replaces, or supersedes them. Do not freeze on an intermediate interpretation; re-evaluate the whole request state after each user turn.\n\n- **Separate \u201clatest update\u201d from \u201centire surviving spec.\u201d** The newest turn tells you what changed, but the final evaluation target is the full accumulated user intent. Verify the assistant against the intersection of all surviving constraints, not just the last message alone.\n\n- **Use user language to build the task spec; do not import assistant phrasing as if it were user intent.** If the assistant introduced extra assumptions, formatting, tie rules, or scope expansions, treat them as non-authoritative unless the user explicitly adopted them later.\n\n- **Treat narrowing language as a scope reduction, not a restatement.** When the user shifts from broad to focused wording, drop obsolete breadth and judge the final answer against the narrowed target. A response can have been reasonable earlier and still become divergent once the task tightens.\n\n- **Diagnose in two passes: first selection/coverage, then projection/output shape.** An assistant can identify the right entities or relationship logic but still fail by returning the wrong fields, the wrong granularity, or extra unsupported material. Keep these checks separate so a correct core does not hide a surface mismatch.\n\n- **Track \u201centity to find\u201d versus \u201csupporting evidence.\u201d** Joins, filters, counts, and other relational logic often serve only to identify the target; they should not be treated as requested output unless the user asked for them explicitly.\n\n- **Treat added fields, summaries, computed columns, and auxiliary metrics as overreach unless the user asked for them.** Extra content is especially suspect after refinement, because it often reflects an obsolete broader interpretation rather than harmless verbosity.\n\n- **Do not count a composite or merged value as satisfying a request for a specific attribute unless that attribute is explicitly present.** Presence in the row is not the same as presence as requested; projection must match the user\u2019s requested granularity.\n\n- **When multiple user constraints survive, treat them as an intersection.** The final target is usually the overlap of all user-stated conditions, not a union of partially related goals. Missing any surviving constraint is a real omission even if the rest are correct.\n\n- **Handle count, ranking, and extremum-style requests by checking what is being compared, not just the presence of a numeric filter.** Verify that the comparison scope matches the user\u2019s stated entity and qualifier; do not accept a neighboring aggregation target as equivalent.\n\n- **Be sensitive to tie and ambiguity handling only when the user resolves it.** If the user has not specified a single-winner or tie-breaking rule, do not invent a mismatch just because multiple results remain valid under the stated contract.\n\n- **Treat negative requests as active constraints.** If the user asks for absence, exclusion, or zero-match behavior, do not reinterpret the task as a positive match search. Positive matches can be a sign of inversion, not success.\n\n- **Flag missing requested items directly and plainly.** When the user asks for multiple fields, entities, or dimensions, omitting any one of them is incomplete even if the rest are correct. Do not bury omissions inside broad \u201cmostly correct\u201d judgments.\n\n- **Prefer precise mismatch labels.** If the answer is too broad, call it overreach or unnecessary expansion; if it misses a newly added requirement, call it omission. Avoid relabeling a scope error as a logic error or vice versa.\n\n- **Preserve structurally valid reasoning in the aligned section even when the final answer has gaps.** If the approach still matches the user\u2019s narrowed goal in some parts, keep that anchor while isolating the remaining divergence. This supports compacted follow-up analysis without discarding useful reasoning.\n\n- **Separate harmless verbosity from substantive divergence.** Extra explanation is not itself a core error unless it changes the requested output shape, introduces unsupported material, or obscures the requested answer.\n\n- **After each refinement, re-check the whole response for scope creep.** Earlier auxiliary details often become wrong once the user narrows the request. The analyzer should actively look for stale columns, stale branches, and stale assumptions that survived from a broader version of the task.\n\n- **Use the latest user wording to resolve ambiguous scope, but do not overcommit beyond it.** When the user refines the ask, update the spec incrementally and avoid inferring additional constraints that were never stated. Keep the task state as small as possible while still satisfying all surviving user requirements.\n\n- **In correction trajectories, compact to the surviving user requirements plus the already-aligned core, and drop exploratory branches.** The goal is to keep the next evaluation anchored on the cleaned target, not on obsolete reasoning or speculative alternatives.",
  "version": 4,
  "history": [
    "",
    "<updated_cheatsheet>\n- **Rebuild the task spec from the latest user turn, then merge earlier user turns as still-valid constraints.** Treat later turns as refinements or narrowing updates, not new independent tasks. Re-check every prior assumption against the newest wording so outdated broader interpretations do not survive.\n\n- **Separate user-stated constraints from assistant-invented inferences.** Only promote a condition into the task spec if the user explicitly said it or the dialogue/schema directly supports it. If support is missing, mark the point as unsupported or uncertain rather than silently baking it into the requirement.\n\n- **Compress incremental disclosure into the smallest complete final requirement.** When the user narrows the target over time, prefer the most specific surviving intent and discard broader earlier interpretations. The final spec should reflect the user\u2019s current objective, not an accumulated superset of all prior variants.\n\n- **Check alignment at the output-shape level, not just at the logic/filter level.** A response can be structurally or semantically related yet still be wrong if it returns the wrong entity, granularity, or projection. Verify separately: what is being returned, what is being filtered, and what aggregation level the user asked for.\n\n- **Flag missing required outputs as concrete gaps.** If the user asks for multiple attributes, entities, or dimensions, a response that omits one of them is incomplete even if the rest is correct. Extra correctness in one area does not compensate for an absent required field, join, or result dimension.\n\n- **Treat \u201cextra\u201d requested content as overreach once the user narrows the ask.** When the latest turn says \u201cjust X,\u201d \u201conly X,\u201d or otherwise restricts scope, additional columns, summaries, or adjacent details should be marked as divergence even if they would be harmless or technically valid.\n\n- **Distinguish row-level filtering from column-level projection.** The analyzer should independently verify whether the assistant got the right subset of records and whether it returned the right information about those records. A correct filter does not justify the wrong output shape, and vice versa.\n\n- **When the user asks for absence/none/zero-match conditions, look for negative evidence patterns and inversion errors.** Responses that enumerate matches or positive cases can be the opposite of what was requested. Preserve the current negative condition and discard prior \u201cfind the matching items\u201d framing that would anchor analysis in the wrong direction.\n\n- **Validate final-query completeness, not just intermediate progress.** If the assistant has only discussed parts of the reasoning, note whether it actually synthesized all surviving constraints into one answer. Intermediate narrowing is useful only if it still supports the final deliverable.\n\n- **Use schema-grounded validation over semantic guessing.** If the available fields do not directly support a concept, any derived proxy should be treated as uncertain and not counted as confirmed alignment. Prefer explicit evidence from the conversation/schema over plausible but unsupported interpretation.\n\n- **Distinguish appropriate caution from spec drift.** Clarifying questions are useful while the task is still underspecified, but once enough information exists, the analyzer should resolve the requirement from the available user turns instead of preserving an unresolved branch.\n\n- **In edit/correction modes, remove misleading exploratory reasoning while keeping validated constraints.** The goal is not to preserve the whole earlier thought process; it is to retain only the joins, filters, and targets that still match the user\u2019s final intent and discard speculative paths that would anchor future reasoning incorrectly.\n\n- **When multiple assistant turns are mostly right, focus on the remaining mismatch.** Do not relitigate earlier acceptable reasoning. Identify the highest-leverage divergence, often an output-schema or granularity error, especially when the assistant is close but not exact.\n\n- **Prefer minimal-valid solutions when the user\u2019s request is narrow.** If the final user wording is concise, the analyzer should favor the smallest answer that satisfies it and treat added adjacent information as likely drift unless the user explicitly asked for it.\n\n- **Track the current entity being returned versus related records used only for filtering.** A response may correctly use related data to determine eligibility but still fail if it returns the wrong object type or wrong level of aggregation. The analyzer should explicitly verify the final deliverable matches the user\u2019s stated target.\n\n- **Keep the task spec outcome-oriented and tied to the user\u2019s final wording.** Build the spec around what the user wants revealed, counted, or checked, then use that spec to test every assistant turn for missing, added, or broadened outputs. Avoid preserving older broadened interpretations once the user has tightened the goal.\n\n- **Mark partially aligned answers as risky when they are incomplete.** Near-miss responses are especially prone to further drift if not corrected. The analyzer should explicitly surface the remaining mismatch rather than treating partial success as full success.\n</updated_cheatsheet>",
    "- **Rebuild the task spec from the latest user turn, then merge earlier user turns only as still-valid constraints.** Treat each new user message as the authoritative update to the contract: later turns refine, narrow, or add constraints, while older broader interpretations should be discarded if they no longer fit. Re-check prior assumptions against the newest wording so stale requirements do not survive by inertia.\n\n- **Use the final accumulated user intent, not the last assistant reply, as the evaluation target.** The analyzer should compare every assistant turn against the current user-defined spec assembled from all user messages. Prior assistant phrasing can become obsolete after incremental disclosure, so it should not anchor what \u201cthe task\u201d is.\n\n- **Separate user-stated constraints from assistant-invented inferences.** Only promote a condition into the spec if the user explicitly said it, or if the dialogue/schema directly supports it. If support is missing, mark it as unsupported or uncertain rather than silently treating it as required.\n\n- **Compress incremental disclosure into the smallest complete surviving requirement.** When the user narrows scope over time, keep only the most specific final intent and drop earlier broader readings. Repeated user requests usually confirm the same constraint rather than create a new task.\n\n- **Treat the latest user wording as a hard constraint update, not a suggestion.** If the user says \u201calso,\u201d \u201cand,\u201d \u201cjust,\u201d \u201conly,\u201d or adds a new value/condition, update the spec to include exactly that change. Do not preserve a broader earlier interpretation once the user has tightened or expanded the target.\n\n- **Check alignment at the output-shape level separately from the logic level.** A response can be directionally correct yet still wrong if it returns the wrong entity, wrong granularity, wrong projection, or wrong aggregation level. Verify independently: what is being returned, what is being filtered, and what shape the final answer has.\n\n- **Distinguish row/entity selection from column/detail selection.** The analyzer should separately confirm whether the assistant identified the right records and whether it returned the right fields about those records. A correct filter does not justify extra columns, and a correct projection does not justify the wrong subset of items.\n\n- **Track the current entity being returned versus related records used only for filtering.** A response may correctly use supporting data to determine eligibility but still fail if it returns the wrong object type or summary level. Explicitly verify that the final deliverable matches the user\u2019s stated target, not just associated evidence.\n\n- **Look for missing required outputs as concrete gaps.** If the user asks for multiple attributes, entities, or dimensions, omitting one is incomplete even if the rest are correct. Partial success should be labeled as incomplete rather than treated as full alignment.\n\n- **Treat extra requested content as overreach once the user narrows the ask.** When the final wording says \u201cjust X,\u201d \u201conly X,\u201d or otherwise restricts scope, added summaries, columns, joins, variants, or adjacent details should be flagged as divergence unless the user explicitly asked for them.\n\n- **Prefer minimal-valid specifications and outputs when the request is narrow.** If the task can be satisfied directly from one table, field, or concise projection, preserve that simpler target instead of letting earlier context pull the analysis toward unnecessary joins, expansions, or auxiliary metrics.\n\n- **Separate \u201cunneeded detail\u201d from \u201cwrong detail.\u201d** Extra information is only a problem when it changes, obscures, or exceeds the user\u2019s requested output. If the assistant is already satisfying the request, mark the extra material as unnecessary expansion rather than as a core error.\n\n- **Use schema-grounded validation over semantic guessing.** If available fields do not directly support a concept, any derived proxy should be treated as uncertain and not counted as confirmed alignment. Prefer explicit evidence from the conversation or schema over plausible but unsupported interpretation.\n\n- **Watch for field drift.** If the conversation centers on one metric, entity, or column, flag when the assistant quietly shifts to a derived or adjacent concept without user support. \u201cHelpful\u201d substitution of a neighboring metric is still a spec mismatch.\n\n- **When the user asks for absence, zero-match, or exclusion conditions, look for negative-evidence patterns and inversion errors.** Responses that enumerate matches or positive cases can be the opposite of what was requested. Preserve the negative condition as the active requirement instead of letting earlier \u201cfind the matching items\u201d framing dominate.\n\n- **Treat intersections and cumulative conditions as exact-set requirements.** When the user adds multiple constraints over time, the final target is often the overlap of all of them, not a union of partial matches. The analyzer should verify that the assistant satisfies every surviving condition together.\n\n- **Validate final-query completeness, not just intermediate reasoning.** Helpful intermediate narrowing only matters if the assistant actually synthesized all surviving constraints into one final answer. Do not reward a response that discusses the right pieces but never combines them correctly.\n\n- **Distinguish appropriate caution from spec drift.** Clarifying questions are useful while the task is underspecified, but once enough user information exists, the analyzer should resolve the requirement from the accumulated turns instead of preserving an unresolved branch.\n\n- **In edit or correction trajectories, keep validated constraints and drop exploratory branches.** The goal is not to preserve the whole earlier thought process; it is to retain only the targets, filters, and outputs that still match the final user intent. Speculative side paths should be removed if they would anchor future analysis on the wrong shape.\n\n- **When multiple assistant turns are mostly right, focus on the remaining mismatch.** Do not relitigate acceptable earlier reasoning. Identify the highest-leverage divergence\u2014often output shape, granularity, or a narrow overreach\u2014so the analyzer can report the real issue cleanly.\n\n- **Prefer issue labels that match the actual divergence.** If the assistant is correct but verbose, call it unnecessary expansion rather than incorrect answer. If the assistant already includes the newly requested element, mark it as already covered instead of inventing a missing requirement.\n\n- **Compact the conversation around the exact surviving requirements for reuse.** In later analysis passes, keep only the aligned core and the current mismatch. Remove irrelevant helper structure and obsolete context so the next step starts from the narrowed, correct target.",
    "- **Start by reconstructing the user\u2019s active contract from all user turns, with the latest turn as the source of new constraints.** Merge earlier user requirements unless the user clearly replaces or narrows them; treat incremental disclosure as additive and re-check stale assumptions against the newest wording.\n\n- **Use the user-derived accumulated spec as the only evaluation target.** Compare every assistant turn against the full surviving user intent, not against prior assistant wording, inferred direction, or an earlier broader interpretation.\n\n- **Treat the latest user message as a hard update, but do not delete earlier valid requirements unless the user actually supersedes them.** The key is to merge constraints across turns, then resolve the final target as the smallest complete set that still satisfies all user-stated conditions.\n\n- **Separate user-stated constraints from assistant-invented support.** Only promote a detail into the spec if the user explicitly requested it or the conversation/schema directly supports it; unsupported additions should be marked as speculative or divergence, not silently accepted.\n\n- **Prefer delta analysis for late-turn changes.** Ask what the newest user message adds, removes, or tightens: fields, filters, exclusions, output shape, or semantics. Flag a mismatch only when the assistant fails to incorporate that delta into the final response.\n\n- **Check both constraint satisfaction and result shape independently.** A response can have the right core logic but still diverge by returning the wrong entity, wrong granularity, wrong projection, too many items, too few items, or the wrong aggregation level.\n\n- **Distinguish core selection from returned detail.** Verify separately whether the assistant identified the right subset of things and whether it returned the right attributes about them. Correct filtering does not justify extra columns, and correct projection does not justify the wrong items.\n\n- **Track the entity or output object being returned versus data used only as support.** A response may rely on appropriate evidence yet still fail if the final deliverable is the wrong type, scope, or summary level.\n\n- **Treat \u201cinclude/add also\u201d as an additive constraint and \u201cjust/only/specifically\u201d as a narrowing constraint.** New user wording may add a required field or tighten semantics; do not preserve a broader earlier interpretation once the user has narrowed the ask.\n\n- **Model \u201cinclude X\u201d as an existence/required-coverage signal unless the conversation says otherwise.** Do not infer extra summaries, counts, joins, or side outputs from a simple inclusion request.\n\n- **Look for omissions as concrete gaps.** If the user asks for multiple fields, entities, or dimensions, missing any one of them is incomplete even if the rest are correct.\n\n- **Treat extra requested content as overreach when the user narrows scope.** Added summaries, auxiliary metrics, alternative branches, or adjacent details become divergence when they exceed the final user request.\n\n- **Separate harmless verbosity from real mismatch.** Extra explanation is not a core error unless it changes the requested output shape, obscures the answer, or adds unsupported material that could steer later turns.\n\n- **Watch for field drift and invented edge-case coverage.** If the assistant shifts to a neighboring metric, surrogate column, or invented mechanism not grounded in the conversation or schema, treat that as a likely spec mismatch even if it seems helpful.\n\n- **Use schema-grounded validation over semantic guessing.** When the data model does not directly support a claimed concept, do not count a plausible proxy as confirmed alignment; mark it as uncertain or unsupported.\n\n- **When the user asks for absence, exclusion, or zero-match behavior, preserve the negative condition as active.** Answers that enumerate positive matches or assume a search-for-matches framing can invert the request.\n\n- **Treat multiple surviving constraints as an intersection, not a union.** If the user adds several conditions over time, the final target is usually the overlap of all of them; verify that the assistant satisfies every surviving condition together.\n\n- **In correction trajectories, keep validated constraints and drop exploratory branches.** Context compaction should preserve the confirmed task spec and aligned reasoning, while removing speculative paths that anchored the wrong interpretation.\n\n- **Prefer issue labels that match the actual divergence.** If the answer is basically correct but too broad, call it overreach or unnecessary expansion; if it misses a newly requested field, call it omission. Do not relabel one kind of mismatch as another.\n\n- **When tie handling is unspecified, do not invent a tie policy error.** Flag ambiguity only when the assistant imposes a rule that conflicts with the user request.\n\n- **After the conversation narrows, judge the final answer against the narrowed target, not the earlier broad one.** A response that was acceptable before refinement can become misaligned if it retains deadweight from obsolete assumptions.\n\n- **If the assistant is partly right, isolate the stable anchor and focus on the remaining divergence.** Re-litigate only what changed; avoid reflagging already-aligned logic just because the answer is not perfectly complete.\n\n- **For reuse and context editing, compact to the surviving user requirements plus the assistant\u2019s aligned elements.** Drop obsolete branches, redundant reasoning, and unsupported embellishments so the next analysis step is anchored on the correct target."
  ],
